        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(ios): native HealthKit read + export to self-hosted webhook",
        "body": "## Summary\n\nAdds an **opt-in, privacy-first Health Export** module to the iOS app: native read-only HealthKit access that forwards selected metrics to a webhook the user runs on their **own private network**. No third-party services, no cloud middleman.\n\nThis is offered as an **optional contribution**. I understand the core declined a built-in HealthKit integration in #27181 — this keeps it fully opt-in, self-hosted, and isolated behind a new Settings screen, so it doesn't change default behavior for anyone who doesn't enable it. If the team would rather not carry this, that's totally fine; happy to keep it as a fork.\n\n## What it does\n\n- **Read-only HealthKit** (`toShare: []`): steps, heart rate, HRV (SDNN), active + basal energy, walking/running distance, sleep, and workouts.\n- **Sleep** (`HKCategorySample`) is aggregated into a `SleepMinutes` metric.\n- **Workouts** use `statistics(for:)` only — **no metadata, no GPS routes** are read.\n- **Units are forced explicitly** per quantity type. HealthKit localizes units by region, which would otherwise make the exported payload schema unstable; forcing units keeps it deterministic.\n- **Anchored reads** (`HKAnchoredObjectQuery`). The anchor advances **only after a successful 2xx POST**, so nothing is silently dropped on a failed upload.\n- **Background delivery** via `HKObserverQuery` + `enableBackgroundDelivery`, plus a `BGProcessingTask` and a reconcile pass when the app returns to foreground.\n- **Anti-exfiltration URL policy:** the webhook URL is validated to `https` + a `.ts.net` (Tailscale MagicDNS) host before it can be saved, so health data can only ever leave the device to a host on the user's own tailnet — never an arbitrary public endpoint. This is an opinionated default for the self-hosted use case and is documented in `HealthExportConfigStore` for maintainers who may want to relax/extend it.\n- **Secrets handling:** the webhook token + URL live in the Keychain (`afterFirstUnlockThisDeviceOnly`); the larger (>4KB) HealthKit anchor lives in an encrypted file, not the Keychain.\n- **Error handling:** 4xx are surfaced to the user without a retry loop (the user must fix token/URL); 5xx use exponential backoff.\n\n## Wiring (minimal, additive)\n\n- `project.yml` / `Info.plist`: link `HealthKit.framework`, add `NSHealthShareUsageDescription` **and** `NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription` (Apple requires both when HealthKit is linked, even read-only — App Store Connect rejects with `90683` otherwise), the `processing` background mode, and the `ai.openclaw.ios.healthexport` BGTask identifier (namespaced to match the existing `ai.openclaw.ios.bgrefresh`).\n- `OpenClaw.entitlements`: `com.apple.developer.healthkit`.\n- `OpenClawApp`: register/observe/schedule the BGTask and reconcile on `.active`.\n- Settings: a new \"Health Export\" row + screen (`HealthExportSettingsView`).\n\nAll product changes are additive — the diff is **+1974 / -0**.\n\n## Tests\n\n`Tests/HealthExportTests.swift` covers URL validation (https + `.ts.net` allow/deny edge cases), payload shaping, sleep aggregation, the anchor-advances-only-on-2xx invariant, and the backoff state machine. Picked up automatically by the `OpenClawTests` target's `Tests/` glob.\n\n`swiftformat --lint` and `swiftlint` (project configs) are clean for the new files.\n\n## Companion server (OSS)\n\nThe webhook that receives the export is a small FastAPI service, MIT-licensed and open source: **`github.com/hernaninverso/openclaw-health`** (Bearer auth on every data endpoint, idempotent ingest, no GPS, loopback/tailnet only). The iOS client and the server are independent — the client just POSTs to whatever `.ts.net` webhook the user configures.\n\nCloses the gap requested in #27181 for users who want it, without making it a core feature.\n",
        "branch": "feat/ios-healthkit-export",
